/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · 2 Chronicles chapters 31-35
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("2chronicles", [
{n:31, name:"Heaps of Tithes", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 31:21",verse:"And in every work that he began in the service of the house of God, and in the law, and in the commandments, to seek his God, he did it with all his heart, and prospered."}, facts:["Did you know the tithes came in so fast that Hezekiah had to order storerooms built in the temple, and the priests said they had eaten their fill with plenty left over?","Did you know the people laid the heaps from the third month to the seventh month, about four months of steady harvest giving?","Did you know the portions reached the priests' little children too, since whole families were counted and cared for in their set office?"], questions:[
{q:"When Hezekiah's reforms began, what did the people of Israel go out and destroy in the cities of Judah?", options:["The images, groves, high places, and altars","The temple gates and courts","The royal storehouses","The walls of the villages"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:1",verse:"Now when all this was finished, all Israel that were present went out to the cities of Judah, and brake the images in pieces, and cut down the groves, and threw down the high places and the altars out of all Judah and Benjamin, in Ephraim also and Manasseh, until they had utterly destroyed them all. Then all the children of Israel returned, every man to his possession, into their own cities.", insight:"Reform began with clearing out the idols, because you cannot serve the LORD and keep the high places too."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah appoint for the priests and the Levites?", options:["Their courses, each man in his service","New robes of fine linen","Lands in the hill country","Houses inside the temple courts"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:2",verse:"And Hezekiah appointed the courses of the priests and the Levites after their courses, every man according to his service, the priests and Levites for burnt offerings and for peace offerings, to minister, and to give thanks, and to praise in the gates of the tents of the LORD.", insight:"Order matters in God's house; Hezekiah gave every servant his appointed place so worship ran faithfully."},
{q:"From what did the king appoint a portion for the daily burnt offerings?", options:["His own substance, the king's portion","The spoil of war","A new tax on the merchants","Gifts from foreign kings"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:3",verse:"He appointed also the king’s portion of his substance for the burnt offerings, to wit, for the morning and evening burnt offerings, and the burnt offerings for the sabbaths, and for the new moons, and for the set feasts, as it is written in the law of the LORD.", insight:"Hezekiah gave first from his own goods, showing that a leader's generosity sets the tone for the whole nation."},
{q:"Why did Hezekiah command the people of Jerusalem to give the priests and Levites their portion?", options:["That they might be encouraged in the law of the LORD","That they might build new houses","That they might travel to teach","That they might store up wealth"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:4",verse:"Moreover he commanded the people that dwelt in Jerusalem to give the portion of the priests and the Levites, that they might be encouraged in the law of the LORD.", insight:"Those who teach the law need freedom from worry, and Hezekiah saw that supporting ministers was part of keeping God's word."},
{q:"What did the people bring in abundance when the command went out?", options:["The firstfruits of corn, wine, oil, and honey, and the tithe of all things","Only gold and silver","Only cattle for sacrifice","Weapons for the army"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:5",verse:"And as soon as the commandment came abroad, the children of Israel brought in abundance the firstfruits of corn, wine, and oil, and honey, and of all the increase of the field; and the tithe of all things brought they in abundantly.", insight:"The people's generosity overflowed the moment they were asked, because a revived heart loves to give."},
{q:"In which months did the people begin and finish laying the heaps of tithes?", options:["They began in the third month and finished in the seventh","They began in the first month and finished in the twelfth","They began at Passover and finished at harvest","They brought them all in one day"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:7",verse:"In the third month they began to lay the foundation of the heaps, and finished them in the seventh month.", insight:"Month after month the gifts kept coming, a steady stream of thankfulness through the whole harvest season."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah and the princes do when they saw the great heaps?", options:["They blessed the LORD, and his people Israel","They ordered the heaps counted and taxed","They sold the surplus abroad","They hid the heaps from enemies"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:8",verse:"And when Hezekiah and the princes came and saw the heaps, they blessed the LORD, and his people Israel.", insight:"Their first response to abundance was worship, blessing God before making any plans for the store."},
{q:"Who questioned the priests and the Levites concerning the heaps?", options:["Hezekiah the king","The chief of the singers","The governor of Jerusalem","The captain of the guard"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:9",verse:"Then Hezekiah questioned with the priests and the Levites concerning the heaps.", insight:"A wise king inspects the work himself; Hezekiah wanted to understand the blessing before organizing it."},
{q:"Which chief priest answered Hezekiah about the heaps?", options:["Azariah, of the house of Zadok","Hilkiah, of the house of Aaron","Eliashib, the scribe","Zadok, the singer"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:10",verse:"And Azariah the chief priest of the house of Zadok answered him, and said, Since the people began to bring the offerings into the house of the LORD, we have had enough to eat, and have left plenty: for the LORD hath blessed his people; and that which is left is this great store.", insight:"Azariah's answer shows God keeps his promises to those who honor him: the givers and the ministers both had plenty."},
{q:"What did Azariah say had happened since the people began bringing offerings?", options:["We have had enough to eat, and have left plenty","The storehouses are nearly empty","The priests have gone hungry","The people have given too little"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:10",verse:"And Azariah the chief priest of the house of Zadok answered him, and said, Since the people began to bring the offerings into the house of the LORD, we have had enough to eat, and have left plenty: for the LORD hath blessed his people; and that which is left is this great store.", insight:"This is the arithmetic of generosity: when God's people gave freely, everyone ate and there was still a great store left."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah command to be prepared in the house of the LORD to hold the offerings?", options:["Chambers to store the tithes and dedicated things","New altars of brass","A second veil for the holy place","Stables for the sacrificial animals"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:11",verse:"Then Hezekiah commanded to prepare chambers in the house of the LORD; and they prepared them,", insight:"Blessing needs stewardship; Hezekiah made room to keep what God had given, faithfully and in order."},
{q:"Which Levite was made ruler over the offerings, tithes, and dedicated things?", options:["Cononiah, and Shimei his brother was next","Kore, the son of Imnah","Azariah, the chief priest","Jehiel, the scribe"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:12",verse:"And brought in the offerings and the tithes and the dedicated things faithfully: over which Cononiah the Levite was ruler, and Shimei his brother was the next.", insight:"Faithful hands were put over holy things, because integrity is as important as generosity."},
{q:"How many overseers are named as serving under Cononiah and Shimei?", options:["Ten","Seven","Twelve","Five"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:13",verse:"And Jehiel, and Azaziah, and Nahath, and Asahel, and Jerimoth, and Jozabad, and Eliel, and Ismachiah, and Mahath, and Benaiah, were overseers under the hand of Cononiah and Shimei his brother, at the commandment of Hezekiah the king, and Azariah the ruler of the house of God.", insight:"The work was shared among many trusted men, so no one bore the burden or the temptation alone."},
{q:"Who was the Levite porter toward the east, set over the freewill offerings?", options:["Kore the son of Imnah","Eden the son of Joah","Miniamin the priest","Shemaiah the scribe"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:14",verse:"And Kore the son of Imnah the Levite, the porter toward the east, was over the freewill offerings of God, to distribute the oblations of the LORD, and the most holy things.", insight:"Even the gatekeeper had a holy trust: distributing God's gifts fairly is itself an act of worship."},
{q:"To whom did Eden, Miniamin, Jeshua, Shemaiah, Amariah, and Shecaniah give portions in the cities of the priests?", options:["To their brethren by courses, both great and small","Only to the chief priests","Only to the singers","To the king's household"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:15",verse:"And next him were Eden, and Miniamin, and Jeshua, and Shemaiah, Amariah, and Shecaniah, in the cities of the priests, in their set office, to give to their brethren by courses, as well to the great as to the small:", insight:"The portions reached great and small alike, because God's provision was never meant for a favored few."},
{q:"From what age were the males counted to receive their daily portion in the house of the LORD?", options:["From three years old and upward","From twelve years old and upward","From twenty years old and upward","From thirty years old and upward"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:16",verse:"Beside their genealogy of males, from three years old and upward, even unto every one that entereth into the house of the LORD, his daily portion for their service in their charges according to their courses;", insight:"Even little ones were reckoned in God's house, showing that children belong in the worshiping community."},
{q:"From what age did the Levites serve in their charges by their courses?", options:["From twenty years old and upward","From thirteen years old and upward","From twenty five years old and upward","From forty years old and upward"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:17",verse:"Both to the genealogy of the priests by the house of their fathers, and the Levites from twenty years old and upward, in their charges by their courses;", insight:"Service had its appointed season; the Levites were counted from the age when they could bear the work."},
{q:"Who was named to give portions to the priests dwelling in the fields of the suburbs?", options:["The men expressed by name in every city","The king's sons","The captains of the army","The merchants of Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:19",verse:"Also of the sons of Aaron the priests, which were in the fields of the suburbs of their cities, in every several city, the men that were expressed by name, to give portions to all the males among the priests, and to all that were reckoned by genealogies among the Levites.", insight:"Known, named men carried the portions to the countryside, so no priest far from the temple was forgotten."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah act throughout all Judah?", options:["He wrought that which was good and right and truth before the LORD his God","He ruled by fear and force","He pleased the princes first","He followed the customs of the nations"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:20",verse:"And thus did Hezekiah throughout all Judah, and wrought that which was good and right and truth before the LORD his God.", insight:"Good, right, and true: three words that sum up a reign God could bless, because character comes before achievement."},
{q:"In every work Hezekiah began, how did he seek his God?", options:["With all his heart, and he prospered","With half his heart, and he struggled","Through others, and he grew weak","Only in trouble, and he was delivered"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 31:21",verse:"And in every work that he began in the service of the house of God, and in the law, and in the commandments, to seek his God, he did it with all his heart, and prospered.", insight:"Wholehearted seeking was the secret of his prosperity; God honors the heart that holds nothing back."}
]},
{n:32, name:"Sennacherib Defeated", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 32:8",verse:"With him is an arm of flesh; but with us is the LORD our God to help us, and to fight our battles. And the people rested themselves upon the words of Hezekiah king of Judah."}, facts:["Did you know the watercourse Hezekiah built to bring Gihon inside the walls is believed to be the Siloam Tunnel, which visitors can still walk through in Jerusalem today?","Did you know Sennacherib's own records boast of trapping Hezekiah in Jerusalem like a bird in a cage, yet never claim to have taken the city?","Did you know Sennacherib was murdered by his own sons while worshiping in the temple of his god, just as this chapter foretells?"], questions:[
{q:"After Hezekiah's reforms, which king came and encamped against the fenced cities of Judah?", options:["Sennacherib king of Assyria","Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria","Necho king of Egypt","Ben-hadad king of Syria"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:1",verse:"After these things, and the establishment thereof, Sennacherib king of Assyria came, and entered into Judah, and encamped against the fenced cities, and thought to win them for himself.", insight:"Faithfulness does not exempt us from trouble; Sennacherib came after the reforms, not before them, to test the nation's trust."},
{q:"What counsel did Hezekiah take with his princes and mighty men?", options:["To stop the waters of the fountains outside the city","To flee to Egypt for horses","To pay tribute at once","To abandon Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:3",verse:"He took counsel with his princes and his mighty men to stop the waters of the fountains which were without the city: and they did help him.", insight:"Hezekiah paired prayer with practical wisdom: trusting God never means neglecting the work our hands can do."},
{q:"Why did the people stop the fountains and the brook that ran through the land?", options:["So the kings of Assyria would not come and find much water","So the fields would not flood","To build a new pool for the temple","To punish the surrounding villages"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:4",verse:"So there was gathered much people together, who stopped all the fountains, and the brook that ran through the midst of the land, saying, Why should the kings of Assyria come, and find much water?", insight:"They denied the enemy every advantage, showing that faith can be both bold and shrewd."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah build and make as he strengthened Jerusalem?", options:["The broken wall, towers, another wall, repaired Millo, and darts and shields in abundance","A new palace and gardens","Ships for the sea","A wall around the whole of Judah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:5",verse:"Also he strengthened himself, and built up all the wall that was broken, and raised it up to the towers, and another wall without, and repaired Millo in the city of David, and made darts and shields in abundance.", insight:"Hezekiah worked as if everything depended on his effort, while trusting as if everything depended on God."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah encourage the people when the Assyrians came?", options:["Be strong and courageous, be not afraid nor dismayed","Flee to the mountains","Hide your treasures","Make peace at any price"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:7",verse:"Be strong and courageous, be not afraid nor dismayed for the king of Assyria, nor for all the multitude that is with him: for there be more with us than with him:", insight:"Courage was his first command, because fear had already done half the enemy's work before a single arrow flew."},
{q:"What contrast did Hezekiah draw between Sennacherib and the LORD?", options:["With him is an arm of flesh; but with us is the LORD our God to help us, and to fight our battles","With him is wisdom; with us is youth","With him is gold; with us is silver","With him is pride; with us is humility"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:8",verse:"With him is an arm of flesh; but with us is the LORD our God to help us, and to fight our battles. And the people rested themselves upon the words of Hezekiah king of Judah.", insight:"This is the heart of the whole chapter: an arm of flesh against the living God is no contest at all."},
{q:"While his servants went to Jerusalem, where did Sennacherib himself lay siege?", options:["Against Lachish","Against Samaria","Against Babylon","Against Tyre"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:9",verse:"After this did Sennacherib king of Assyria send his servants to Jerusalem, (but he himself laid siege against Lachish, and all his power with him,) unto Hezekiah king of Judah, and unto all Judah that were at Jerusalem, saying,", insight:"The great king was busy elsewhere, yet his threats still reached Jerusalem; bluster does not need to be nearby to frighten."},
{q:"What did Sennacherib's servants claim Hezekiah was persuading the people to do?", options:["To give themselves over to die by famine and by thirst","To rebel against the priests","To worship the gods of Assyria","To open the gates at night"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:11",verse:"Doth not Hezekiah persuade you to give over yourselves to die by famine and by thirst, saying, The LORD our God shall deliver us out of the hand of the king of Assyria?", insight:"The enemy twisted Hezekiah's faith into foolishness, which is how lies usually work: a half truth aimed at trust."},
{q:"What did the Assyrians mock about Hezekiah removing the high places?", options:["That he had taken away the people's altars and told them to worship before one altar","That he had stolen the temple gold","That he had closed the temple doors","That he had banished the singers"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:12",verse:"Hath not the same Hezekiah taken away his high places and his altars, and commanded Judah and Jerusalem, saying, Ye shall worship before one altar, and burn incense upon it?", insight:"They mistook obedience for weakness; tearing down idols looked like destroying worship to eyes that never knew the true God."},
{q:"What boast did Sennacherib's servants make about the gods of other nations?", options:["That no god of any nation or kingdom had delivered his people out of the Assyrian hand","That the gods of Assyria were the oldest gods","That their gods had made the heavens","That the gods fought for the highest bidder"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:15",verse:"Now therefore let not Hezekiah deceive you, nor persuade you on this manner, neither yet believe him: for no god of any nation or kingdom was able to deliver his people out of mine hand, and out of the hand of my fathers: how much less shall your God deliver you out of mine hand?", insight:"Their argument was history without God in it, and it collapsed the moment it met the God who writes history."},
{q:"In what language did the Assyrians cry out to frighten the people on the wall?", options:["In the Jews' speech, so all could understand","In the Assyrian tongue only","In Egyptian, to confuse them","In signs, because they were mute"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:18",verse:"Then they cried with a loud voice in the Jews’ speech unto the people of Jerusalem that were on the wall, to affright them, and to trouble them; that they might take the city.", insight:"Fear speaks our language on purpose; the enemy wanted every ear on the wall to hear and tremble."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah and the prophet Isaiah do when the blasphemy came?", options:["They prayed and cried to heaven","They sent gold to Sennacherib","They fled the city by night","They answered the taunts themselves"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:20",verse:"And for this cause Hezekiah the king, and the prophet Isaiah the son of Amoz, prayed and cried to heaven.", insight:"They took the insults straight to heaven instead of answering them on the wall, and heaven answered back."},
{q:"What did the LORD send against the Assyrian camp?", options:["An angel, which cut off all the mighty men of valour, and the leaders and captains","A plague of locusts","A flood that drowned the army","An earthquake that split the camp"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:21",verse:"And the LORD sent an angel, which cut off all the mighty men of valour, and the leaders and captains in the camp of the king of Assyria. So he returned with shame of face to his own land. And when he was come into the house of his god, they that came forth of his own bowels slew him there with the sword.", insight:"One angel did what no army of Judah could do, proving that the battle was the LORD's all along."},
{q:"How did Sennacherib die after returning home with shame?", options:["They that came forth of his own bowels slew him with the sword in the house of his god","He died of old age in his palace","He drowned crossing the river","He was stoned by his people"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:21",verse:"And the LORD sent an angel, which cut off all the mighty men of valour, and the leaders and captains in the camp of the king of Assyria. So he returned with shame of face to his own land. And when he was come into the house of his god, they that came forth of his own bowels slew him there with the sword.", insight:"The man who mocked the living God fell by the sword in the temple of his dead one, a grim and fitting end."},
{q:"When Hezekiah was sick unto death, what happened after he prayed?", options:["The LORD spake unto him, and gave him a sign","He died within three days","The physicians cured him","Isaiah refused to visit him"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:24",verse:"In those days Hezekiah was sick to the death, and prayed unto the LORD: and he spake unto him, and he gave him a sign.", insight:"Prayer turned even a death sentence into a testimony, and God added a sign to his mercy."},
{q:"What went wrong in Hezekiah's heart after his healing?", options:["His heart was lifted up, and he rendered not again according to the benefit done unto him","He forgot the law entirely","He turned to idols","He refused to pray again"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:25",verse:"But Hezekiah rendered not again according to the benefit done unto him; for his heart was lifted up: therefore there was wrath upon him, and upon Judah and Jerusalem.", insight:"Blessing became his danger; a lifted heart forgets the Giver faster than trouble ever did."},
{q:"How did Hezekiah respond to the pride in his heart?", options:["He humbled himself, with the inhabitants of Jerusalem","He blamed the priests","He doubled his treasures","He ignored the warning"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:26",verse:"Notwithstanding Hezekiah humbled himself for the pride of his heart, both he and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, so that the wrath of the LORD came not upon them in the days of Hezekiah.", insight:"Humility came before the wrath fell, and that timing made all the difference for him and his city."},
{q:"For what did Hezekiah build treasuries?", options:["Silver, gold, precious stones, spices, shields, and pleasant jewels","Only weapons of war","Grain for the poor","Foreign idols"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:27",verse:"And Hezekiah had exceeding much riches and honour: and he made himself treasuries for silver, and for gold, and for precious stones, and for spices, and for shields, and for all manner of pleasant jewels;", insight:"God gave him riches and honor, and he stored them with order, a reminder that prosperity is a trust, not a trophy."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah do with the upper watercourse of Gihon?", options:["He stopped it and brought it down to the west side of the city of David","He dammed it to flood the valley","He gave it to the Assyrians","He turned it into a moat"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:30",verse:"This same Hezekiah also stopped the upper watercourse of Gihon, and brought it straight down to the west side of the city of David. And Hezekiah prospered in all his works.", insight:"The famous tunnel secured Jerusalem's water in a siege; faith prepared for the worst while hoping in God."},
{q:"Why did God leave Hezekiah to himself when the ambassadors of Babylon came?", options:["To try him, that he might know all that was in his heart","To punish him at once","Because God had forgotten him","To make him king of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 32:31",verse:"Howbeit in the business of the ambassadors of the princes of Babylon, who sent unto him to enquire of the wonder that was done in the land, God left him, to try him, that he might know all that was in his heart.", insight:"Even a good king gets tested; God let the moment reveal what pride had hidden, so the heart could be known and healed."}
]},
{n:33, name:"Manasseh Repents", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 33:13",verse:"And prayed unto him: and he was intreated of him, and heard his supplication, and brought him again to Jerusalem into his kingdom. Then Manasseh knew that the LORD he was God."}, facts:["Did you know Manasseh's fifty and five years is the longest reign of any king of Judah, good or bad?","Did you know Manasseh is the only king of Judah ever carried captive to a foreign land and then brought home to his throne?","Did you know Manasseh's prayer was recorded among the sayings of the seers, a book that has not survived to our day?"], questions:[
{q:"How old was Manasseh when he began to reign, and how long did he reign?", options:["Twelve years old, and he reigned fifty and five years","Eight years old, and he reigned thirty one years","Twenty two years old, and he reigned two years","Thirty years old, and he reigned sixteen years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:1",verse:"Manasseh was twelve years old when he began to reign, and he reigned fifty and five years in Jerusalem:", insight:"Fifty five years, the longest reign in Judah, shows how long God can wait and still how fully a sinner can be restored."},
{q:"What kind of reign did Manasseh begin?", options:["Evil in the sight of the LORD, like the abominations of the heathen","Righteous like his father Hezekiah","Peaceful and uneventful","Devoted to the temple service"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:2",verse:"But did that which was evil in the sight of the LORD, like unto the abominations of the heathen, whom the LORD had cast out before the children of Israel.", insight:"A godly father does not guarantee a godly son; each heart must choose for itself."},
{q:"What did Manasseh rebuild that his father Hezekiah had broken down?", options:["The high places, with altars for Baalim and groves","The city walls","The temple porch","The royal palace"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:3",verse:"For he built again the high places which Hezekiah his father had broken down, and he reared up altars for Baalim, and made groves, and worshipped all the host of heaven, and served them.", insight:"In one generation he rebuilt what took a revival to tear down, a warning about how quickly idols return."},
{q:"Where did Manasseh dare to build altars?", options:["In the house of the LORD, where God said his name would be for ever","On the Mount of Olives only","In the wilderness","In the houses of his princes"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:4",verse:"Also he built altars in the house of the LORD, whereof the LORD had said, In Jerusalem shall my name be for ever.", insight:"He put idols in the very house named for God, defiance at its boldest and its blindest."},
{q:"What terrible thing did Manasseh do in the valley of the son of Hinnom?", options:["He caused his children to pass through the fire","He built a fortress there","He hid the temple treasures","He planted a grove"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:6",verse:"And he caused his children to pass through the fire in the valley of the son of Hinnom: also he observed times, and used enchantments, and used witchcraft, and dealt with a familiar spirit, and with wizards: he wrought much evil in the sight of the LORD, to provoke him to anger.", insight:"Child sacrifice was the depth of his evil, proof that idolatry always ends in cruelty."},
{q:"Besides idolatry, what occult practices did Manasseh use?", options:["Observing times, enchantments, witchcraft, a familiar spirit, and wizards","Only dreams and visions","Astrology for farming","Nothing else is named"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:6",verse:"And he caused his children to pass through the fire in the valley of the son of Hinnom: also he observed times, and used enchantments, and used witchcraft, and dealt with a familiar spirit, and with wizards: he wrought much evil in the sight of the LORD, to provoke him to anger.", insight:"He traded God's voice for every counterfeit spirit available, and each one led him further from the truth."},
{q:"What image did Manasseh set in the house of God?", options:["A carved image, the idol which he had made","A golden calf","An image of Baal only","A statue of himself"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:7",verse:"And he set a carved image, the idol which he had made, in the house of God, of which God had said to David and to Solomon his son, In this house, and in Jerusalem, which I have chosen before all the tribes of Israel, will I put my name for ever:", insight:"The house built for God's name held a carving of man's making; nothing shows a fall like an idol in the sanctuary."},
{q:"How did Manasseh's influence compare with the heathen nations?", options:["He made Judah do worse than the heathen whom the LORD had destroyed","He kept Judah better than the heathen","He had no influence at all","He drove the heathen out"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:9",verse:"So Manasseh made Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem to err, and to do worse than the heathen, whom the LORD had destroyed before the children of Israel.", insight:"God's people can sink lower than pagans when they know the truth and still choose the lie."},
{q:"When the LORD spoke to Manasseh and his people, how did they respond?", options:["They would not hearken","They repented at once","They asked for a sign","They fled to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:10",verse:"And the LORD spake to Manasseh, and to his people: but they would not hearken.", insight:"God warned before he judged; the tragedy is not that they never heard, but that they would not listen."},
{q:"How did the LORD bring Manasseh down?", options:["By the captains of the king of Assyria, who took him among the thorns, bound him with fetters, and carried him to Babylon","By a plague in Jerusalem","By his own servants in the palace","By the king of Egypt at Megiddo"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:11",verse:"Wherefore the LORD brought upon them the captains of the host of the king of Assyria, which took Manasseh among the thorns, and bound him with fetters, and carried him to Babylon.", insight:"The proud king was dragged away in chains, yet even those chains became the road back to God."},
{q:"What did Manasseh do when he was in affliction?", options:["He besought the LORD his God, and humbled himself greatly","He cursed God and died","He bribed his captors","He worshiped his idols more"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:12",verse:"And when he was in affliction, he besought the LORD his God, and humbled himself greatly before the God of his fathers,", insight:"Affliction did what prosperity never could: it bent the hardest heart in Judah toward heaven."},
{q:"What did Manasseh learn when God heard his prayer and brought him back to Jerusalem?", options:["Then Manasseh knew that the LORD he was God","That Babylon was stronger than Judah","That his idols had saved him","That prayer was useless"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:13",verse:"And prayed unto him: and he was intreated of him, and heard his supplication, and brought him again to Jerusalem into his kingdom. Then Manasseh knew that the LORD he was God.", insight:"This is the turning point of the chapter and of his life: knowledge of God born not from teaching but from mercy received."},
{q:"After his return, what building work did Manasseh do?", options:["A wall without the city of David, on the west side of Gihon to the fish gate, compassing Ophel","A new temple for Baal","A palace in Babylon","Nothing at all"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:14",verse:"Now after this he built a wall without the city of David, on the west side of Gihon, in the valley, even to the entering in at the fish gate, and compassed about Ophel, and raised it up a very great height, and put captains of war in all the fenced cities of Judah.", insight:"Restored grace showed itself in restored duty; the forgiven king went back to serving his city."},
{q:"What did Manasseh do with the strange gods and the idol?", options:["He took them away and cast them out of the city","He hid them in the temple","He sold them to merchants","He melted them for tribute"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:15",verse:"And he took away the strange gods, and the idol out of the house of the LORD, and all the altars that he had built in the mount of the house of the LORD, and in Jerusalem, and cast them out of the city.", insight:"True repentance throws the idols out of the city, not into a closet for later."},
{q:"What did Manasseh command Judah to do after repairing the altar of the LORD?", options:["To serve the LORD God of Israel","To keep the high places","To serve the king of Assyria","To pay double tribute"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:16",verse:"And he repaired the altar of the LORD, and sacrificed thereon peace offerings and thank offerings, and commanded Judah to serve the LORD God of Israel.", insight:"The convert became a preacher; the man who led Judah into sin now led them back to God."},
{q:"What compromise remained among the people after Manasseh's reforms?", options:["They still sacrificed in the high places, yet unto the LORD their God only","They returned to Baal entirely","They stopped all sacrifices","They worshiped in Babylon"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:17",verse:"Nevertheless the people did sacrifice still in the high places, yet unto the LORD their God only.", insight:"Old habits linger even after revival; the people kept the high places though their hearts had turned."},
{q:"Where was Manasseh's prayer recorded?", options:["Among the sayings of the seers","On the temple walls","In the king's own diary only","It was never written down"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:19",verse:"His prayer also, and how God was intreated of him, and all his sin, and his trespass, and the places wherein he built high places, and set up groves and graven images, before he was humbled: behold, they are written among the sayings of the seers.", insight:"God treasured the prayer of a repentant tyrant enough to have it written down, a hope for every great sinner."},
{q:"How old was Amon when he began to reign, and how long did he reign?", options:["Two and twenty years old, and he reigned two years","Twelve years old, and he reigned fifty five years","Eight years old, and he reigned thirty one years","Twenty five years old, and he reigned eleven years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:21",verse:"Amon was two and twenty years old when he began to reign, and reigned two years in Jerusalem.", insight:"Two short years show that a reign is measured by direction, not duration."},
{q:"How did Amon differ from his father Manasseh in the matter of humility?", options:["He humbled not himself as his father had, but trespassed more and more","He humbled himself even more","He repented in Babylon too","He never sinned at all"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:23",verse:"And humbled not himself before the LORD, as Manasseh his father had humbled himself; but Amon trespassed more and more.", insight:"Amon saw his father's repentance and refused it; the same mercy offered becomes judgment when it is despised."},
{q:"What did the people of the land do after Amon was slain?", options:["They slew all that had conspired against him, and made Josiah his son king","They fled to Egypt","They crowned the chief conspirator","They left the throne empty"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 33:25",verse:"But the people of the land slew all them that had conspired against king Amon; and the people of the land made Josiah his son king in his stead.", insight:"The people kept the line of David intact, and out of Amon's dark two years came Josiah, the boy reformer."}
]},
{n:34, name:"The Book Found", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 34:27",verse:"Because thine heart was tender, and thou didst humble thyself before God, when thou heardest his words against this place, and against the inhabitants thereof, and humbledst thyself before me, and didst rend thy clothes, and weep before me; I have even heard thee also, saith the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know Josiah was only sixteen when he began seeking God, and twenty when he started smashing idols across the land?","Did you know Huldah is one of only a handful of women in the Old Testament called a prophetess, and the king's top officials went to her for God's word?","Did you know the book of the law had been lost inside the temple itself, showing how far Judah had drifted from God's word?"], questions:[
{q:"How old was Josiah when he began to reign, and how long did he reign?", options:["Eight years old, and he reigned one and thirty years","Twelve years old, and he reigned fifty five years","Twenty years old, and he reigned sixteen years","Thirty years old, and he reigned twenty nine years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:1",verse:"Josiah was eight years old when he began to reign, and he reigned in Jerusalem one and thirty years.", insight:"God put a child on the throne and made him a reformer, proof that youth is no barrier to wholehearted obedience."},
{q:"In whose ways did Josiah walk?", options:["In the ways of David his father, declining neither to the right hand nor to the left","In the ways of Manasseh his grandfather","In the ways of the kings of Israel","In his own ways"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:2",verse:"And he did that which was right in the sight of the LORD, and walked in the ways of David his father, and declined neither to the right hand, nor to the left.", insight:"He skipped past his father and grandfather and reached back to David, choosing his model rather than inheriting it."},
{q:"In the eighth year of his reign, being yet young, what did Josiah begin to do?", options:["He began to seek after the God of David his father","He began to build palaces","He began to gather armies","He began to trade with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:3",verse:"For in the eighth year of his reign, while he was yet young, he began to seek after the God of David his father: and in the twelfth year he began to purge Judah and Jerusalem from the high places, and the groves, and the carved images, and the molten images.", insight:"Revival started in a teenager's private seeking, years before a single idol fell; public reform grows from secret devotion."},
{q:"In the twelfth year of his reign, what did Josiah begin to purge?", options:["Judah and Jerusalem from the high places, groves, carved images, and molten images","The temple treasury of old debts","The army of foreign soldiers","The palace of evil counselors"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:3",verse:"For in the eighth year of his reign, while he was yet young, he began to seek after the God of David his father: and in the twelfth year he began to purge Judah and Jerusalem from the high places, and the groves, and the carved images, and the molten images.", insight:"Four years of seeking led to action; true devotion eventually picks up the hammer."},
{q:"What did Josiah do with the dust of the broken idols?", options:["He strowed it upon the graves of them that had sacrificed unto them","He threw it into the river","He sold it to the merchants","He buried it under the temple"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:4",verse:"And they brake down the altars of Baalim in his presence; and the images, that were on high above them, he cut down; and the groves, and the carved images, and the molten images, he brake in pieces, and made dust of them, and strowed it upon the graves of them that had sacrificed unto them.", insight:"Grinding idols to dust and scattering it on their worshipers' graves was his way of saying the lie was dead."},
{q:"What did Josiah do with the bones of the idol priests?", options:["He burnt them upon their altars","He buried them with honor","He sent them to Babylon","He left them untouched"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:5",verse:"And he burnt the bones of the priests upon their altars, and cleansed Judah and Jerusalem.", insight:"The grim act fulfilled old prophecy and cleansed the land, showing that God takes false worship seriously."},
{q:"How far did Josiah's cleansing reach beyond Judah?", options:["To the cities of Manasseh, Ephraim, Simeon, even unto Naphtali","Only to Jerusalem","Only to Bethlehem","To Egypt and Assyria"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:6",verse:"And so did he in the cities of Manasseh, and Ephraim, and Simeon, even unto Naphtali, with their mattocks round about.", insight:"His zeal crossed old tribal borders; the revival was not a local project but a national one."},
{q:"In the eighteenth year of his reign, whom did Josiah send to repair the house of the LORD?", options:["Shaphan, Maaseiah the governor, and Joah the recorder","Hilkiah, Azariah, and Zadok","The captains of the army","The singers and porters"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:8",verse:"Now in the eighteenth year of his reign, when he had purged the land, and the house, he sent Shaphan the son of Azaliah, and Maaseiah the governor of the city, and Joah the son of Joahaz the recorder, to repair the house of the LORD his God.", insight:"Eighteen years in, the young reformer turned from tearing down to building up, because repair is the other half of revival."},
{q:"From whom had the Levites gathered the money brought into the house of God?", options:["From Manasseh and Ephraim, the remnant of Israel, Judah, and Benjamin","From the king of Assyria","From Egyptian traders","From the temple tax only"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:9",verse:"And when they came to Hilkiah the high priest, they delivered the money that was brought into the house of God, which the Levites that kept the doors had gathered of the hand of Manasseh and Ephraim, and of all the remnant of Israel, and of all Judah and Benjamin; and they returned to Jerusalem.", insight:"The offering came from a reunited people, north and south together, giving for God's house as one nation."},
{q:"What did the workmen buy with the money?", options:["Hewn stone and timber for couplings, to floor the houses","Gold for new idols","Weapons for the guard","Silk for the priests' robes"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:11",verse:"Even to the artificers and builders gave they it, to buy hewn stone, and timber for couplings, and to floor the houses which the kings of Judah had destroyed.", insight:"The gifts became stone and timber in faithful hands, turning generosity into a standing, usable temple."},
{q:"How did the men do the work, and who were the overseers?", options:["They did it faithfully, overseen by Jahath, Obadiah, Zechariah, and Meshullam","They did it slowly, with no overseers","They refused to work","They worked only at night"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:12",verse:"And the men did the work faithfully: and the overseers of them were Jahath and Obadiah, the Levites, of the sons of Merari; and Zechariah and Meshullam, of the sons of the Kohathites, to set it forward; and other of the Levites, all that could skill of instruments of musick.", insight:"Faithful work under faithful overseers, including Levites skilled in music, shows that excellence belongs in God's service."},
{q:"What did Hilkiah the priest find in the house of the LORD?", options:["A book of the law of the LORD given by Moses","A chest of gold","An old sword of David","A hidden letter from Isaiah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:14",verse:"And when they brought out the money that was brought into the house of the LORD, Hilkiah the priest found a book of the law of the LORD given by Moses.", insight:"God's word had been lost inside God's own house; finding it again was the true beginning of Josiah's reformation."},
{q:"To whom did Hilkiah deliver the book?", options:["To Shaphan the scribe","To the king directly","To Huldah the prophetess","To the chief singer"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:15",verse:"And Hilkiah answered and said to Shaphan the scribe, I have found the book of the law in the house of the LORD. And Hilkiah delivered the book to Shaphan.", insight:"The book passed hand to hand with care, because those who find God's word become responsible to share it."},
{q:"What did Shaphan do with the book when he brought it to the king?", options:["He read it before the king","He hid it from the king","He burned it","He sold it for silver"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:18",verse:"Then Shaphan the scribe told the king, saying, Hilkiah the priest hath given me a book. And Shaphan read it before the king.", insight:"Reading Scripture aloud to the king put God's word above the throne, where it belongs."},
{q:"What did Josiah do when he heard the words of the law?", options:["He rent his clothes","He laughed","He ordered a feast","He went back to sleep"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:19",verse:"And it came to pass, when the king had heard the words of the law, that he rent his clothes.", insight:"Torn clothes meant a torn heart; conviction, not curiosity, is the right response to hearing God's law."},
{q:"Why did Josiah say the wrath of the LORD was poured out upon them?", options:["Because our fathers have not kept the word of the LORD","Because the temple was too small","Because the Assyrians were strong","Because the priests were poor"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:21",verse:"Go, enquire of the LORD for me, and for them that are left in Israel and in Judah, concerning the words of the book that is found: for great is the wrath of the LORD that is poured out upon us, because our fathers have not kept the word of the LORD, to do after all that is written in this book.", insight:"Josiah owned a guilt he did not personally commit, which is what true repentance often looks like: weeping over sins we inherited."},
{q:"Who was Huldah, to whom the king's men went?", options:["A prophetess, the wife of Shallum the keeper of the wardrobe","The queen mother","A singer in the temple","A servant in the palace"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:22",verse:"And Hilkiah, and they that the king had appointed, went to Huldah the prophetess, the wife of Shallum the son of Tikvath, the son of Hasrah, keeper of the wardrobe; (now she dwelt in Jerusalem in the college:) and they spake to her to that effect.", insight:"When the king needed God's word, he sent his highest officials to a woman, because God gives his Spirit to whom he wills."},
{q:"What did the LORD say he would bring upon Jerusalem?", options:["All the curses written in the book, because they had forsaken him","A great harvest","Peace for a hundred years","A new temple"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:24",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, Behold, I will bring evil upon this place, and upon the inhabitants thereof, even all the curses that are written in the book which they have read before the king of Judah:", insight:"The warnings were not empty threats but written curses coming due; God's word means what it says."},
{q:"Why did God promise to hear Josiah's prayer?", options:["Because his heart was tender, and he humbled himself, rent his clothes, and wept","Because he was young","Because he was rich","Because he threatened the prophets"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:27",verse:"Because thine heart was tender, and thou didst humble thyself before God, when thou heardest his words against this place, and against the inhabitants thereof, and humbledst thyself before me, and didst rend thy clothes, and weep before me; I have even heard thee also, saith the LORD.", insight:"A tender heart moves God more than a strong army; humility turned the sentence into mercy."},
{q:"What did Josiah do in the house of the LORD with all the people?", options:["He read in their ears all the words of the book of the covenant","He crowned his son","He counted the treasures","He dismissed the priests"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 34:30",verse:"And the king went up into the house of the LORD, and all the men of Judah, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, and the priests, and the Levites, and all the people, great and small: and he read in their ears all the words of the book of the covenant that was found in the house of the LORD.", insight:"The king became a preacher, putting the whole nation under the sound of God's word at once."}
]},
{n:35, name:"Josiah's Passover", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 35:18",verse:"And there was no passover like to that kept in Israel from the days of Samuel the prophet; neither did all the kings of Israel keep such a passover as Josiah kept, and the priests, and the Levites, and all Judah and Israel that were present, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem."}, facts:["Did you know Josiah's Passover was the greatest since the days of Samuel, centuries before any of the kings?","Did you know the king personally supplied thirty thousand lambs and kids plus three thousand bullocks so every family could keep the feast?","Did you know Jeremiah's lament for Josiah became a lasting ordinance, sung by choirs in Israel long after the king's death?"], questions:[
{q:"On what day did they kill the passover in Josiah's great celebration?", options:["The fourteenth day of the first month","The tenth day of the seventh month","The first day of the year","The fifteenth day of the third month"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:1",verse:"Moreover Josiah kept a passover unto the LORD in Jerusalem: and they killed the passover on the fourteenth day of the first month.", insight:"Keeping the feast on God's appointed day showed that revival meant returning to Scripture's calendar, not inventing a new one."},
{q:"What did Josiah do for the priests?", options:["He set them in their charges and encouraged them to the service of the house of the LORD","He dismissed them all","He sent them to Babylon","He made them soldiers"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:2",verse:"And he set the priests in their charges, and encouraged them to the service of the house of the LORD,", insight:"Encouragement is part of leadership; the priests served better because their king believed in their work."},
{q:"What did Josiah tell the Levites to do with the holy ark?", options:["Put it in the house which Solomon built; it shall not be a burden upon your shoulders","Carry it into battle","Hide it in the wilderness","Take it to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:3",verse:"And said unto the Levites that taught all Israel, which were holy unto the LORD, Put the holy ark in the house which Solomon the son of David king of Israel did build; it shall not be a burden upon your shoulders: serve now the LORD your God, and his people Israel,", insight:"The ark belonged in its resting place, and the Levites were freed from carrying it to serve God and the people instead."},
{q:"According to whose writings were the Levites to prepare themselves by their courses?", options:["According to the writing of David and of Solomon his son","According to the writing of Moses only","According to the king's new decree","According to the Assyrian records"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:4",verse:"And prepare yourselves by the houses of your fathers, after your courses, according to the writing of David king of Israel, and according to the writing of Solomon his son.", insight:"Josiah anchored the celebration in David and Solomon's order, because true worship is received, not improvised."},
{q:"How many lambs and kids, and how many bullocks, did Josiah give the people from his own substance?", options:["Thirty thousand lambs and kids, and three thousand bullocks","Ten thousand lambs and one thousand bullocks","Five thousand lambs and five hundred bullocks","One hundred thousand of each"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:7",verse:"And Josiah gave to the people, of the flock, lambs and kids, all for the passover offerings, for all that were present, to the number of thirty thousand, and three thousand bullocks: these were of the king’s substance.", insight:"The king's staggering generosity meant no poor family missed the Passover for lack of a lamb."},
{q:"What did Hilkiah, Zechariah, and Jehiel give the priests for the passover offerings?", options:["Two thousand and six hundred small cattle, and three hundred oxen","Gold and silver only","Nothing at all","Only unleavened bread"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:8",verse:"And his princes gave willingly unto the people, to the priests, and to the Levites: Hilkiah and Zechariah and Jehiel, rulers of the house of God, gave unto the priests for the passover offerings two thousand and six hundred small cattle, and three hundred oxen.", insight:"The rulers of God's house gave willingly, and leaders who give first make generosity contagious."},
{q:"What did Conaniah and the chief of the Levites give the Levites?", options:["Five thousand small cattle, and five hundred oxen","Ten thousand sheep","Only money","Nothing; they kept it all"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:9",verse:"Conaniah also, and Shemaiah and Nethaneel, his brethren, and Hashabiah and Jeiel and Jozabad, chief of the Levites, gave unto the Levites for passover offerings five thousand small cattle, and five hundred oxen.", insight:"The Levites provided for their own brethren, a beautiful picture of ministers caring for ministers."},
{q:"In the passover service, what did the priests and the Levites each do?", options:["The priests sprinkled the blood, and the Levites flayed the offerings","The priests sang, and the Levites danced","The priests cooked, and the Levites ate","They all did the same work"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:11",verse:"And they killed the passover, and the priests sprinkled the blood from their hands, and the Levites flayed them.", insight:"Each group kept its God given task, and the shared work moved smoothly because everyone knew their place."},
{q:"How was the passover roasted, and how were the other holy offerings cooked?", options:["The passover was roasted with fire; the other offerings were sod in pots, caldrons, and pans","Everything was boiled","Everything was eaten raw","The passover was boiled and the rest roasted"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:13",verse:"And they roasted the passover with fire according to the ordinance: but the other holy offerings sod they in pots, and in caldrons, and in pans, and divided them speedily among all the people.", insight:"Even the cooking followed the ordinance; in true worship, the details matter because God gave them."},
{q:"Why did the Levites prepare food for themselves and for the priests afterward?", options:["Because the priests were busied in offering until night","Because the Levites were lazy","Because the king commanded a fast","Because there was no food left"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:14",verse:"And afterward they made ready for themselves, and for the priests: because the priests the sons of Aaron were busied in offering of burnt offerings and the fat until night; therefore the Levites prepared for themselves, and for the priests the sons of Aaron.", insight:"Those who serve others all day still need to eat; the Levites' care for the weary priests is ministry behind the scenes."},
{q:"What did the singers and the porters do during the feast?", options:["The singers stayed in their place and the porters waited at every gate","They all went home early","They traded places for the day","They left the temple empty"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:15",verse:"And the singers the sons of Asaph were in their place, according to the commandment of David, and Asaph, and Heman, and Jeduthun the king’s seer; and the porters waited at every gate; they might not depart from their service; for their brethren the Levites prepared for them.", insight:"Nobody abandoned his post, not even to eat; faithfulness in small duties made the great day possible."},
{q:"Besides the passover, what feast did the people keep, and for how long?", options:["The feast of unleavened bread, seven days","The feast of trumpets, one day","The feast of tabernacles, eight days","No other feast"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:17",verse:"And the children of Israel that were present kept the passover at that time, and the feast of unleavened bread seven days.", insight:"A full week of unleavened bread extended the celebration, because one day is rarely enough for gratitude."},
{q:"How did Josiah's passover compare with all others since Samuel?", options:["There was no passover like it in Israel from the days of Samuel the prophet","It was the smallest on record","It was only for the priests","It was forbidden by the prophets"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:18",verse:"And there was no passover like to that kept in Israel from the days of Samuel the prophet; neither did all the kings of Israel keep such a passover as Josiah kept, and the priests, and the Levites, and all Judah and Israel that were present, and the inhabitants of Jerusalem.", insight:"Centuries of kings had come and gone, yet none kept the feast like this boy king; wholeheartedness outshines long opportunity."},
{q:"In what year of Josiah's reign was this passover kept?", options:["The eighteenth year","The eighth year","The twelfth year","The thirty first year"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:19",verse:"In the eighteenth year of the reign of Josiah was this passover kept.", insight:"The same year the book was found, the nation feasted on its meaning; discovered truth quickly became celebrated truth."},
{q:"After the temple was prepared, which king came up to fight against Carchemish by Euphrates?", options:["Necho king of Egypt","Sennacherib king of Assyria","Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon","Tiglath-pileser king of Assyria"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:20",verse:"After all this, when Josiah had prepared the temple, Necho king of Egypt came up to fight against Charchemish by Euphrates: and Josiah went out against him.", insight:"Josiah's peaceful years ended with a foreign war on his doorstep, a reminder that faithfulness does not cancel every danger."},
{q:"What warning did Necho send to Josiah?", options:["Forbear thee from meddling with God, who is with me, that he destroy thee not","Surrender Jerusalem at once","Pay tribute of gold","Join me against Babylon"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:21",verse:"But he sent ambassadors to him, saying, What have I to do with thee, thou king of Judah? I come not against thee this day, but against the house wherewith I have war: for God commanded me to make haste: forbear thee from meddling with God, who is with me, that he destroy thee not.", insight:"Strange as it sounds, God spoke through a pagan king, and Josiah's refusal to listen cost him dearly."},
{q:"What did Josiah do instead of turning back, and where did he fight?", options:["He disguised himself and came to fight in the valley of Megiddo","He fled to the mountains","He made peace with Necho","He waited in Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:22",verse:"Nevertheless Josiah would not turn his face from him, but disguised himself, that he might fight with him, and hearkened not unto the words of Necho from the mouth of God, and came to fight in the valley of Megiddo.", insight:"Disguise could not hide him from God's word ignored; the battle was lost before the first arrow flew."},
{q:"What happened to Josiah at Megiddo?", options:["The archers shot him, and he said, Have me away; for I am sore wounded","He won a great victory","He was taken captive","He escaped unhurt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:23",verse:"And the archers shot at king Josiah; and the king said to his servants, Have me away; for I am sore wounded.", insight:"The reformer's life ended on a battlefield he should never have entered, a sobering end to a shining reign."},
{q:"Where did Josiah die and get buried, and how did the nation respond?", options:["He was brought to Jerusalem, died, was buried with his fathers, and all Judah and Jerusalem mourned","He died in Egypt and was forgotten","He died at Megiddo and was buried there alone","He recovered and reigned on"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:24",verse:"His servants therefore took him out of that chariot, and put him in the second chariot that he had; and they brought him to Jerusalem, and he died, and was buried in one of the sepulchres of his fathers. And all Judah and Jerusalem mourned for Josiah.", insight:"The whole nation mourned, because even a flawed ending could not erase thirty one years of faithful service."},
{q:"Who lamented for Josiah, and what became of the lament?", options:["Jeremiah lamented, and the singing men and women made it an ordinance in Israel","No one mourned him","Only his family wept","The Egyptians wrote the lament"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 35:25",verse:"And Jeremiah lamented for Josiah: and all the singing men and the singing women spake of Josiah in their lamentations to this day, and made them an ordinance in Israel: and, behold, they are written in the lamentations.", insight:"Jeremiah's tears became Israel's song; a godly life echoes in the grief and gratitude of those left behind."}
]}
]);
